"[2509.11101v1] EmoBench-Reddit: A Hierarchical Benchmark for Evaluating the Emotional Intelligence of Multimodal Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11101v1)\nciteturn3184view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11101v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 162\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: A newer version of this paper has been withdrawn by Li Haokun\nL9: \nL10: [Submitted on 14 Sep 2025 (this version), latest version 23 Sep 2025 (cite5†v3 )]\nL11: # Title:EmoBench-Reddit: A Hierarchical Benchmark for Evaluating the Emotional Intelligence of Multimodal Large Language Models\nL12: \nL13: Authors:cite6†Haokun Li , cite7†Yazhou Zhang , cite8†Jizhi Ding , cite9†Qiuchi Li , cite10†Peng Zhang L14: \nL15: View a PDF of the paper titled EmoBench-Reddit: A Hierarchical Benchmark for Evaluating the Emotional Intelligence of Multimodal Large Language Models, by Haokun Li and 4 other authors\nL16: \nL17: cite11†View PDF cite12†HTML (experimental) L18: > Abstract:With the rapid advancement of Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs), they have demonstrated exceptional capabilities across a variety of vision-language tasks. However, current evaluation benchmarks predominantly focus on objective visual question answering or captioning, inadequately assessing the models' ability to understand complex and subjective human emotions. To bridge this gap, we introduce EmoBench-Reddit, a novel, hierarchical benchmark for multimodal emotion understanding.\nL19: The dataset comprises 350 meticulously curated samples from the social media platform Reddit, each containing an image, associated user-provided text, and an emotion category (sad, humor, sarcasm, happy) confirmed by user flairs. We designed a hierarchical task framework that progresses from basic perception to advanced cognition, with each data point featuring six multiple-choice questions and one open-ended question of increasing difficulty.\nL20: Perception tasks evaluate the model's ability to identify basic visual elements (e.g., colors, objects), while cognition tasks require scene reasoning, intent understanding, and deep empathy integrating textual context. We ensured annotation quality through a combination of AI assistance (Claude 4) and manual verification.\nL21: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite13†arXiv:2509.11101 [cs.CL]\nL23:    | (or cite14†arXiv:2509.11101v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL24:    | cite15†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.11101†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Li Haokun [cite16†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Sun, 14 Sep 2025 05:40:24 UTC (16 KB)\nL29: cite17†[v2] Mon, 22 Sep 2025 08:45:20 UTC (1 KB) (withdrawn)\nL30: cite5†[v3] Tue, 23 Sep 2025 02:12:08 UTC (15 KB)\nL31: \nL32: Full-text links:\nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled EmoBench-Reddit: A Hierarchical Benchmark for Evaluating the Emotional Intelligence of Multimodal Large Language Models, by Haokun Li and 4 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite11†View PDF L38:   * cite12†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite18†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite19†view license L42: \nL43: ### Current browse context:\nL44: \nL45: cs.CL\nL46: \nL47: cite20†< prev |   cite21†next > L48: \nL49: cite22†new | cite23†recent | cite24†2025-09 L50: \nL51: Change to browse by:\nL52: \nL53: cite25†cs L54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite26†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite27†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L58:   * cite28†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.or