Search Tools\nL134: \nL135: [Input] Link to Influence Flower\nL136: \nL137: Influence Flower (cite42†What are Influence Flowers?†influencemap.cmlab.dev )\nL138: \nL139: [Input] Core recommender toggle\nL140: \nL141: CORE Recommender (cite43†What is CORE?†core.ac.uk )\nL142: \nL143:   * Author\nL144:   * Venue\nL145:   * Institution\nL146:   * Topic\nL147: \nL148: [Input] About arXivLabs\nL149: # arXivLabs: experimental projects with community collaborators\nL150: \nL151: arXivLabs is a framework that allows collaborators to develop and share new arXiv features directly on our website.\nL152: \nL153: Both individuals and organizations that work with arXivLabs have embraced and accepted our values of openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\nL154: Have an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? cite44†Learn more about arXivLabs†info.arxiv.org .\nL155: \nL156: cite45†Which authors of this paper are endorsers? | Disable MathJax (cite46†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org )\nL157: \nL158: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite47†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\nL159: cite48†About†info.arxiv.org · cite49†Help†info.arxiv.org · cite50†Contact†info.arxiv.org · cite51†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite52†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite53†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite54†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite55†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L160: \nL161: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEmoBench-Reddit: A Hierarchical Benchmark for Evaluating the Emotional Intelligence of Multimodal Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.11101v1)\nciteturn3184view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2509.11101v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 356\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Related Work L18:     1. cite8†2.1 MLLM Evaluation Benchmarks L19:     2. cite9†2.2 Exploring and Evaluating Emotional Intelligence L20:     3. cite10†2.3 The Perception-Cognition Gap L21:     4. cite11†2.4 Datasets from Social Media L22:   4. cite12†3 EmoBench-Reddit Dataset Construction L23:     1. cite13†3.1 Data Source and Filtering L24:     2. cite14†3.2 Dataset Structure and Task Design L25:       1. cite15†A. Perception Tasks L26:       2. cite16†B. Cognition Tasks L27:     3. cite17†3.3 Annotation and Quality Control L28:   5. cite18†4 Evaluation Methodology L29:     1. cite19†4.1 Tasks and Metrics L30:     2. cite20†4.2 Evaluation Strategies L31:   6. cite21†5 Experimental Setup L32:     1. cite22†5.1 Models Evaluated L33:     2. cite23†5.2 Experimental Environment L34:   7. cite24†6 Results and Analysis L35:     1. cite25†6.1 Overall Performance Overview L36:     2. cite26†6.2 Task-Level Analysis: The Perception-Cognition Gap L37:     3. cite27†6.3 Emotion-Type Analysis: Sarcasm as an Insurmountable Hurdle L38:   8. cite28†7 Discussion and Future Work L39:     1. cite29†Limitations: L40:     2. cite30†Future Work: L41:   9. cite31†8 Conclusion L42:   10. cite32†References L43: cite33†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L44: \nL45: arXiv:2509.11101v1 [cs.CL] 14 Sep 2025\nL46: # EmoBench-Reddit: A Hierarchical Benchmark for Evaluating the Emotional Intelligence of Multimodal Large Language Models\nL47: \nL48: Haokun Li Affiliation: Tianjin University    Yazhou Zhang^{*} Affiliation: Tianjin University    Jizhi Ding Affiliation: Shandong Institute of Petroleum and Chemical Technology    Qiuchi Li Affiliation: Beijing Institute of Technology    Peng Zhang^{*} Affiliation: Tianjin University\nL49: ###### Abstract\nL50: With the rapid advancement of Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs), they have demonstrated exceptional capabilities across a variety of vision-language tasks. However, current evaluation benchmarks predominantly focus on objective visual question answering or captioning, inadequately assessing the models’ ability to understand complex and subjective human emotions. To bridge this gap, we introduce EmoBench-Reddit, a novel, hierarchical benchmark for multimodal emotion understanding.\nL51: The dataset comprises 350 meticulously curated samples from the social media platform Reddit, each containing an image, associated user-provided text, and an emotion category (sad, humor, sarcasm, happy) confirmed by user flairs. We designed a hierarchical task framework that progresses from basic perception to advanced cognition, with each data point featuring six multiple-choice questions and one open-ended question of increasing difficulty.\nL52: Perception tasks evaluate the model’s ability to identify basic visual elements (e.g., colors, objects), while cognition tasks require scene reasoning, intent understanding, and deep empathy integrating textual context. We ensured annotation quality through a combination of AI assistance (Claude 4) and manual verification.\nL53: We conducted a comprehensive evaluation of nine leading MLLMs, including GPT-5, Gemini-2.5-pro, and GPT-4o, on EmoBench-Reddit. The results reveal several key characteristics of current models: 1) Top-tier models, represented by GPT-5 and Gemini-2.5-pro, exhibit a leading edge in most tasks, especially showing stable performance on basic perception and elementary cognition.\nL54: 2) All models perform well on basic perception tasks, but their performance drops significantly on advanced cognition tasks, indicating that the leap from \"seeing\" to \"understanding\" remains a formidable challenge. 3) The complexity of emotion types significantly impacts model performance; emotions like \"sarcasm,\" which require deep contextual and cultural understanding, are the biggest hurdle for all models, whereas \"happy\" and \"humor\" are relatively easier to process.\nL55: 4) Models with smaller parameter counts show clear capability bottlenecks when handling complex cognitive tasks. This study not only provides an effective tool for assessing the emotional intelligence of MLLMs but also points to critical directions for future model development, namely, strengthening deep reasoning and contextualized understanding of complex human emotions.\nL56: ## 1 Introduction\nL57: In recent years, Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have achieved breakthrough progress. Their ability to process and understand information from different modalities, such as text, images, and audio, has demonstrated powerful capabilities in domains like image captioning cite34†Chen et al. (2015) , visual question answering cite35†Antol et al. (2015) , and cross-modal retrieval.\nL58: The emergence of models like GPT-4V cite36†OpenAI (2023) and Gemini cite37†Google (2023) has further propelled the development of Artificial General Intelligence (AGI), enabling more natural and intelligent human-computer interactions.\nL59: However, the current MLLM evaluation landscape primarily focuses on the understanding and description of objective facts. Benchmarks such as MME cite38†Fu et al. (2023) and MMBench cite39†Liu et al. (2023) measure multimodal capabilities by assessing performance on tasks like OCR, object recognition, and spatial relationship judgment. While necessary, these evaluations largely overlook a more advanced and core aspect of intelligence: Emotional Intelligence (EI).\nL60: EI—the ability to perceive, understand, manage, and utilize emotions—is key to achieving true human-machine empathy and harmonious interaction. A model incapable of understanding a user’s subtle emotions, such as sarcasm or implicit sadness, will have its application scenarios severely limited.\nL61: To address the lack of depth in emotion understanding within current evaluation systems, we argue for a benchmark that can systematically and hierarchically assess the emotional intelligence of MLLMs. Such a benchmark should possess the following characteristics:\nL62: \nL63:   1. 1.\nL64: \nL65: Data Authenticity: Data should originate from real-world scenarios, not lab environments, to reflect the complexity and diversity of human emotional expression.\nL66: \nL67:   2. 2.\nL68: Task Hierarchy: Evaluation tasks should progress from simple visual perception to complex cognitive reasoning, allowing for precise diagnosis of where a model’s capabilities fall short.\nL69: \nL70:   3. 3.\nL71: \nL72: Emotional Diversity: It should cover a range of typical human emotions, especially those reliant on complex context and social knowledge, such as sarcasm.\nL73: \nL74:   4. 4.\nL75: Comprehensive Evaluation: It should combine multiple-choice and open-ended questions to ensure both objective assessment and an evaluation of the accuracy and richness of generated content.\nL76: Based on these considerations, we constructed EmoBench-Reddit, a hierarchical benchmark specifically designed to evaluate the emotional intelligence of MLLMs. We chose Reddit as our data source due to its vast repository of user-generated image-text content, where \"flairs\" are often used to tag a post’s theme or emotion, providing a natural source of high-quality, labeled data.\nL77: Our dataset contains 350 samples covering four emotions: \"sad,\" \"humor,\" \"sarcasm,\" and \"happy.\" We have meticulously designed a three-tiered framework progressing from \"Perception\" to \"Cognition\" to systematically analyze the entire process, from a model seeing the image content to understanding its underlying emotional intent.\nL78: Our main contributions are as follows:\nL79: \nL80:   * •\nL81: \nL82: We propose and construct EmoBench-Reddit: A novel, hierarchical multimodal emotion understanding benchmark sourced from a real-world social media context.\nL83: \nL84:   * •\nL85: \nL86: We design an innovative evaluation framework: This framework includes multi-level tasks from low-level perception to high-level cognition, enabling a fine-grained assessment of a model’s emotional intelligence.\nL87: \nL88:   * •\nL89: We conduct a comprehensive model evaluation: We systematically evaluated 9 mainstream MLLMs, revealing their strengths and common weaknesses in emotion understanding.\nL90: \nL91:   * •\nL92: \nL93: We provide profound insights: The experimental results clearly indicate the severe deficiencies of current MLLMs in handling complex, context-dependent emotions (especially sarcasm) and validate that perception is the foundation of cognition.\nL94: The remainder of this paper is organized as follows: Section 2 reviews related work. Section 3 details the construction of the EmoBench-Reddit dataset. Section 4 explains our evaluation methods and metrics. Section 5 describes the experimental setup. Section 6 presents and analyzes the results. Section 7 discusses limitations and future work. Section 8 concludes the paper.\nL95: ## 2 Related Work\nL96: ### 2.1 MLLM Evaluation Benchmarks\nL97: With the rise of MLLMs, numerous benchmarks have been developed. Early benchmarks like VQAv2 cite40†Goyal et al. (2017) focused on open-ended visual question answering. To provide a more comprehensive evaluation, subsequent benchmarks became more integrated and fine-grained. For instance, MME cite38†Fu et al. (2023) proposed a framework covering 14 sub-tasks across perception and cognition, though its cognitive tasks still lean towards factual reasoning. MMBench cite39†Liu et al.\nL98: (2023) used ChatGPT to generate multiple-choice questions, creating a \"potpourri\" style benchmark with 20 capability dimensions but lacking in-depth analysis of specific advanced abilities. SEED-Bench cite41†Li et al. (2023) focused on compositional reasoning over multimodal content. While these benchmarks have greatly advanced MLLM development, they generally lack a dedicated and deep evaluation of the subjective and complex dimension of emotion.\nL99: ### 2.2 Exploring and Evaluating Emotional Intelligence\nL100: Emotional Intelligence (EI) is a key component of human intelligence and plays a central role in achieving natural and harmonious human-computer interaction. However, in the field of MLLM evaluation, attention to EI has lagged. Some works have begun to incorporate emotional factors. For example, LL-Bench cite42†Liu et al. (2024) built a large-scale multimodal benchmark that includes tasks like emotion recognition and cause inference, but its task design is relatively basic, primarily at the perception level.\nL101: DeepEval cite43†Yang et al. (2024) offers a progressive multimodal evaluation framework that implicitly requires emotion understanding (e.g., for sarcasm and humor), but EI is not its core evaluation target, and it lacks a systematic hierarchy for emotional capabilities. More inspiring is the work by cite44†Zhang et al. (2023) , which proposed a three-stage framework mimicking human thought processes to bridge the gap between semantic understanding and model reasoning, though it remains in a conceptual phase.\nL102: ### 2.3 The Perception-Cognition Gap\nL103: Existing benchmarks, including those mentioned above, collectively reveal a critical challenge: a significant gap between \"Perception\" and \"Cognition\" in MLLMs. Perception refers to the model’s ability to identify and describe explicit, objective elements in an image (e.g., objects, scenes, actions, text), an area where current MLLMs perform relatively well.\nL104: Cognition, on the other hand, requires the model to perform deep reasoning, interpretation, and prediction based on perceived information, integrating background knowledge, common sense, context, and even socio-cultural factors. This involves grasping abstract and subjective content like intent, emotion, metaphor, and sarcasm, which is a common weakness in current MLLMs.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMALLM: Multi-Agent Large Language Models Framework (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.11656v1)\nciteturn3184view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2509.11656v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 539\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLatest News from Google Research Blog - Google Research - 2025 (https://research.google/blog/2025/09/)\nciteturn3184view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://research.google/blog/2025/09/\",\"lineno\":190}); Total lines: 210\nL0: cite0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com L1: ## Explore our many areas of focus\nL2: \nL3: cite1†Explore all research areas L4: \nL5: Applied AI & sciences\nL6: \nL7: cite2†Earth AI cite3†Health AI cite4†Science AI cite5†Sustainability & crisis resilience L8: \nL9: Foundational ML & algorithms\nL10: \nL11: cite6†Algorithms & theory cite7†Information retrieval cite8†Machine intelligence cite9†Machine perception cite10†Natural language processing L12: \nL13: People, systems & quantum AI\nL14: cite11†Human-computer interaction and visualization cite12†Networking cite13†Quantum AI cite14†Responsible AI cite15†Anti abuse cite16†Software engineering cite17†Software systems L15: \nL16: Learn More\nL17: \nL18: cite18†Publications cite19†Projects L